{'_client': <twikit.client.client.Client object at 0x000001E534E2AE40>, '_data': {'__typename': 'Tweet', 'rest_id': '1877408331196670209', 'core': {'user_results': {'result': {'__typename': 'User', 'id': 'VXNlcjoxMTE1NzQ5NjMzOTg3MzA1NDcy', 'rest_id': '1115749633987305472', 'affiliates_highlighted_label': {}, 'has_graduated_access': True, 'is_blue_verified': True, 'profile_image_shape': 'Circle', 'legacy': {'can_dm': True, 'can_media_tag': True, 'created_at': 'Tue Apr 09 22:53:37 +0000 2019', 'default_profile': True, 'default_profile_image': False, 'description': 'Retired Royal Canadian Marine Search & Rescue Specialist. Happily married to a compassionate and dedicated frontline Doctor with 3  amazing, talented children.', 'entities': {'description': {'urls': []}}, 'fast_followers_count': 0, 'favourites_count': 197141, 'followers_count': 11915, 'friends_count': 9798, 'has_custom_timelines': True, 'is_translator': False, 'listed_count': 8, 'location': '', 'media_count': 5181, 'name': 'Barry Sharp', 'normal_followers_count': 11915, 'pinned_tweet_ids_str': ['1878838948962951297'], 'possibly_sensitive': False, 'profile_banner_url': 'https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_banners/1115749633987305472/1722786870', 'profile_image_url_https': 'https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1820126123474538496/wurAA1jZ_normal.jpg', 'profile_interstitial_type': '', 'screen_name': 'BarryESharp', 'statuses_count': 199826, 'translator_type': 'none', 'verified': False, 'want_retweets': False, 'withheld_in_countries': []}}}}, 'unmention_data': {}, 'edit_control': {'edit_tweet_ids': ['1877408331196670209'], 'editable_until_msecs': '1736447601000', 'is_edit_eligible': False, 'edits_remaining': '5'}, 'is_translatable': False, 'views': {'count': '30', 'state': 'EnabledWithCount'}, 'source': '<a href="http://twitter.com/download/iphone" rel="nofollow">Twitter for iPhone</a>', 'note_tweet': {'is_expandable': True, 'note_tweet_results': {'result': {'id': 'Tm90ZVR3ZWV0OjE4Nzc0MDgzMzA5NTc2ODA2NDA=', 'text': "Report on the Efficacy of mRNA and DNA Vaccines Against the B.1.351 Variant \n\nThe emergence of the B.1.351 variant of SARS-CoV-2, known as the Beta variant, raised significant concerns during 2020 regarding the efficacy of mRNA and DNA vaccines. This variant was characterized by a range of immune escape mutations that notably compromised vaccine effectiveness. Alarmingly, B.1.351 was circulating widely prior to the public rollout of these vaccines, rendering the manufacturers' clinical trials largely irrelevant.\n\nThe B.1.351 variant introduced key mutations, including E484K and K417N in the spike protein, which played a crucial role in diminishing the neutralizing capacity of antibodies generated by the mRNA and DNA vaccines. Research has shown a substantial decrease in neutralization titers—some studies report reductions of 6 to 12-fold against B.1.351 compared to earlier strains, indicating a marked decline in vaccine efficacy.\n\nBy the time these vaccines were being administered on a large scale, B.1.351 had already been classified as a Variant of Concern (VOC) by the World Health Organization in December 2020, highlighting its widespread presence and the urgent challenge it posed to vaccine effectiveness.\n\nThe K417N mutation, first identified in B.1.351, has since appeared in the Omicron variant (B.1.1.529), underscoring the persistent challenge of immune escape and raising concerns about the vaccines' effectiveness against new strains.\n\nClaims suggesting that these vaccines mitigate the severity of illness or reduce hospitalization rates in individuals infected with variants like B.1.351 largely stem from observational studies. These studies are inherently limited in their ability to establish causality and are often confounded by various factors such as age, pre-existing health conditions, and shifts in public health measures, which may skew interpretations of vaccine effectiveness.\n\nWhile the introduction of mRNA and DNA vaccines were touted as  a significant advancement in public health, their efficacy faced immediate challenges from variants like B.1.351. The immune escape mutations in these variants not only reduce vaccine effectiveness but also reveal the discrepancies between controlled clinical trial environments and real-world scenarios. This situation underscores the critical need for adaptive vaccine development strategies that can keep pace with viral evolution, and calls for a more cautious and nuanced interpretation of vaccine efficacy data derived from observational studies. These facts open up legal issues around coercion and mandates applied from August 2021.", 'entity_set': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'urls': [], 'user_mentions': []}}}}, 'legacy': {'bookmark_count': 0, 'bookmarked': False, 'created_at': 'Thu Jan 09 17:33:21 +0000 2025', 'conversation_id_str': '1877347490887811322', 'display_text_range': [14, 293], 'entities': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'timestamps': [], 'urls': [], 'user_mentions': [{'id_str': '69156861', 'name': 'Chief Nerd', 'screen_name': 'TheChiefNerd', 'indices': [0, 13]}]}, 'favorite_count': 0, 'favorited': False, 'full_text': '@TheChiefNerd Report on the Efficacy of mRNA and DNA Vaccines Against the B.1.351 Variant \n\nThe emergence of the B.1.351 variant of SARS-CoV-2, known as the Beta variant, raised significant concerns during 2020 regarding the efficacy of mRNA and DNA vaccines. This variant was characterized by', 'in_reply_to_screen_name': 'TheChiefNerd', 'in_reply_to_status_id_str': '1877347490887811322', 'in_reply_to_user_id_str': '69156861', 'is_quote_status': False, 'lang': 'en', 'quote_count': 0, 'reply_count': 0, 'retweet_count': 0, 'retweeted': False, 'user_id_str': '1115749633987305472', 'id_str': '1877408331196670209'}}, '_legacy': {'bookmark_count': 0, 'bookmarked': False, 'created_at': 'Thu Jan 09 17:33:21 +0000 2025', 'conversation_id_str': '1877347490887811322', 'display_text_range': [14, 293], 'entities': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'time